<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Golden Bull of Sicily</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1212">1212</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-bull-of-1356">Golden Bull</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a> was a charter issued in Basel in 1212 by Frederick II, then King of Sicily and claimant to the German throne. It confirmed the hereditary royal title of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottokar-i-of-bohemia">Ottokar I of Bohemia</a> and his successors and granted the Bohemian kings privileges within the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a>, including the right to invest <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/golden-bull-of-sicily.jpg" alt="Golden Bull of Sicily" width="320" height="454" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: emperor Frederick II Original uploader was Pastorius at cs.wikipedia · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Golden_Bull_of_Sicily.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Bohemian rulers had been made kings before, but not for good. Emperor Henry IV raised the Přemyslid duke Vratislaus II to kingship at the diet of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mainz">Mainz</a> in 1085, but after his death in 1092 his brother Conrad I ruled again as duke. Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-barbarossa">Frederick Barbarossa</a> gave the title in 1158 to Vladislaus II, who had joined his Italian campaign against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a>, but Vladislaus could not secure the succession of his eldest son, Frederick.</p><p>In 1198 Frederick's half-brother Ottokar I exploited the rivalry between the two men elected King of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a>, Otto IV of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-welf">House of Welf</a> and the Hohenstaufen Philip of Swabia. Philip gave him a hereditary royal title for his support, and Ottokar won recognition from Otto IV and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-innocent-iii">Pope Innocent III</a> as well. After Philip's assassination and the papal ban on Otto IV in 1210, Ottokar changed sides again: in 1211 he and several princes met at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nuremberg">Nuremberg</a> and elected the young Frederick II as alium imperatorem, the other emperor. On his way to Germany Frederick issued the bull at Basel on 26 September 1212.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>The bull made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moravia">Moravia</a> an autonomous and indivisible part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a>. The Bohemian king was no longer appointed by the emperor and had to attend only those <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imperial-diet">imperial diets</a> held near the Bohemian border. He was to be the premier prince-elector of the Empire and to provide every future emperor with a bodyguard of 300 knights for his journey to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> to be crowned. Frederick also declared that he and the Empire would grant the investiture of Bohemia only to a ruler approved by the people of the country.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The kingship gave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a> an exceptional place within the Empire. When Charles IV held both Bohemia and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> in 1346, he established the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lands-of-the-bohemian-crown">Crown of Bohemia</a>, whose lands remained outside the Empire's suzerainty and were not counted as Imperial States. For the 800th anniversary of its signing, the document was shown to the public at the National Archive for four days in September 2012.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Golden Bull of Sicily” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1212 the young Frederick II, seeking allies against his rival Otto IV, issued the <b>Golden Bull of Sicily</b>, which made the royal title hereditary, recognised the right of the Bohemians to choose their own king, whom the emperor would then confirm, and limited the king’s obligations to…</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-bohemia">Kingdom of Bohemia · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The arrangement did not last: Conrad Otto himself became duke of Bohemia in 1189, and once the Bohemian rulers had secured a hereditary royal title, confirmed by the <b>Golden Bull of Sicily</b> in 1212, Moravia settled into its lasting position as a margraviate held under the Bohemian crown.</blockquote><a href="/empire/margraviate-of-moravia">Margraviate of Moravia · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1198 Ottokar I secured a hereditary royal crown, confirmed by the <b>Golden Bull of Sicily</b> in 1212, and the duchy became the Kingdom of Bohemia, a leading state of the Empire.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-bohemia">Duchy of Bohemia</a></li></ul></section>
